meta {
  name: Import Lab Tests (Excel)
  type: http
  seq: 21
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/master-data/{{masterDataId}}/lab-tests/import-xlsx
  body: multipartForm
  auth: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{businessAccessToken}}
}

body:multipart-form {
  file: @file(C:\path\to\lab-tests-export.xlsx)
}

vars:pre-request {
  masterDataId: 3bebbd8d-eef4-4bfa-88f3-3aff4a4a4516
}

tests {
  test("201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });
  test("reports created/updated/skipped", function () {
    const data = res.getBody() && res.getBody().data;
    expect(data).to.have.property("created");
    expect(data).to.have.property("updated");
    expect(data).to.have.property("skipped");
    expect(data.skipped).to.be.an("array");
  });
}

docs {
  Upload an edited `.xlsx` workbook (downloaded from **Export Lab Tests
  (Excel)**, or hand-built to the same layout) to bulk **upsert** lab tests —
  the counterpart to the JSON-rows **Bulk Import Lab Tests** endpoint above,
  but this one supports updates and nested samples/result
  parameters/reference ranges/values, and reads a real `.xlsx` file instead
  of pre-parsed JSON.

  Form field name is **`file`**. Only
  `application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet`
  (`.xlsx`) is accepted; anything else → 400/422 `INVALID_UPLOAD_FILE`. Max
  size matches the generic attachment cap (10 MB).

  ## Expected file format

  ONE worksheet named **`Lab Tests`**, blank row 1, a 120-column header row
  2, then data starting row 3. 117 of those columns are the exact list from
  the business team's reference format — several labels intentionally
  repeat across the Result Parameter / Reference Range / Reference Value
  blocks, so columns are matched by **position**, not unique header text. 3
  columns are additions on top of that reference format — **"Mandatory
  Department"** (right after "Mandatory Test") and **"Repeat Interval
  Value"**/**"Repeat Interval Unit"** (right after "Repeat Interval
  Restriction") — added because the reference format had no way to satisfy
  `assertCoreInvariants` when the paired flag is Yes; see **Export Lab Tests
  (Excel)** above for why.

  A test occupies **one or more rows**:
  - Test-level scalar columns (Test Name → Test Status, the Notes block) are
    read only from the row where **Test Name is non-blank** — that row
    starts a new test; every row after it with a blank Test Name is a
    **continuation row** of that same test.
  - **Mandatory Department** and **Repeat Interval Value**/**Repeat Interval
    Unit** are read like any other test-level scalar (from the test's first
    row only). Mandatory Department is resolved name→id the same way
    Department is; it's **required** (422 `LAB_TEST_IMPORT_VALIDATION_FAILED`,
    message "Mandatory Department is required when Mandatory Test is Yes")
    whenever "Mandatory Test" is Yes. Repeat Interval Value+Unit are likewise
    both required whenever "Repeat Interval Restriction" is Yes.
  - **Samples** are read positionally: the sample columns on the test's Nth
    row belong to its Nth sample.
  - **Result Parameters** (and their Reference Ranges/Values) are read as
    **contiguous blocks**: a non-blank "Parameter Name" cell starts a new
    parameter and claims every row below it (with a blank Parameter Name)
    until the next non-blank "Parameter Name" or the test's row-span ends.
    Each of those rows may also carry one Reference Range and/or one
    Reference Value for that parameter.
  - **Version Control** columns are read for round-trip display only and are
    **ignored** on import (version history is managed elsewhere, never
    bulk-edited via Excel).

  The exported file's test-level and per-parameter-own columns are vertically
  merged and center-aligned for readability (see **Export Lab Tests
  (Excel)** above). A merged Excel cell reports its value on every row of the
  merge (via `getCell()`), so a continuation row's Test Name / Parameter Name
  cell would otherwise look non-blank too — the parser explicitly detects
  this (a cell whose merge master lives on an earlier row) and treats it as
  blank BEFORE grouping rows into tests/parameters, so it behaves exactly
  like an unmerged file with a genuinely blank continuation cell. **If a
  user unmerges a Test Name or Parameter Name cell and retypes the same
  text into every row**, that text is no longer a merge continuation — the
  file now genuinely looks like two separate tests/parameters with the same
  name, and import correctly rejects it as a duplicate (not a parsing bug;
  don't unmerge those two columns). Samples and Reference Ranges/Values are
  never merged in the export and are always read strictly per-row.

  ## Matching (create vs. update)

  A test matches an existing **active** test in this master data by
  **Test Code** (case-insensitive); no match creates a new test. There are
  no hidden id columns in this format.

  **Samples** have no natural key in the xlsx format — on every update, the
  uploaded file's Samples become the complete new set for that test
  (existing ones soft-deleted, the uploaded set inserted fresh). Nothing
  outside this test holds a reference to a `LabTestSample` id, so this is
  safe.

  **Result Parameters** are different: `LabReportResultValue`,
  `CriticalAlert`, and `OutOfRangeFlag` all hold a logical reference to
  `LabTestResultParam.id`, so blindly recreating parameters on every
  re-import would silently orphan any existing lab report tied to one. To
  prevent that, a parameter matches an existing **active** one on the same
  test by **Parameter Code** (case-insensitive):
  - **Matched** → its `id` is preserved; its own scalar fields are patched
    in place, and its Reference Ranges/Values are fully replaced (those
    have no natural key, so full-replace is safe there).
  - **Unmatched** (a new Parameter Code) → a new parameter is created.
  - **An existing active parameter whose code is absent from the upload**
    → soft-deleted (its id and history remain intact for anything still
    referencing it; it just drops off the live test).

  ## Behaviour

  - **PARTIAL import — every test is validated independently.** Each
    assembled test is checked on its own: structural (types/required/enum),
    catalogue-name lookups (Department/Category/Sub Category, case-
    insensitive), cross-field invariants (price ordering, mandatory-dept,
    repeat-interval, calc-formula-required), and duplicate `testName`/
    `testCode`/`parameterCode`. A test with **any** error is **skipped**
    (not saved) — but every OTHER valid test in the same file is still
    created/updated in one transaction. A duplicate name/code found between
    two tests in the same file skips **both** of them (there's no way to
    tell which one the user meant to keep).
  - Response is always `200` (never a rejection for a per-test problem) →
    `{ "created": <count>, "updated": <count>, "skipped": [ { "rowLabel":
    "Row N" | "Rows N-M", "errors": ["<reason>", ...] }, ... ] }`. `skipped`
    is `[]` on a fully clean import.
  - The ONE exception: a **file-level structural failure** — the upload
    isn't a readable `.xlsx`, the `Lab Tests` sheet or a required column is
    missing, or there are no data rows at all — has no single test to blame
    and still rejects the whole file as 422
    `LAB_TEST_IMPORT_VALIDATION_FAILED` with **nothing** saved, since
    there's no per-row context left to partially trust.

  **Every error message names the Excel column, never the internal DTO
  field.** A raw class-validator message always embeds the DTO's camelCase
  property name (e.g. `"priceMsrp must be an integer number"`, or an
  `@IsEnum` failure listing raw enum members like `SINGLE_STEP`) — a
  spreadsheet user has no way to map that back to a column or a value they'd
  actually type. `LabTestService.humanizeValidationMessage` rewrites every
  such message before it's returned: the field name becomes its Excel header
  (`"Price MSRP must be an integer number"`), and any raw enum member in the
  message becomes the display label the sheet uses (`"must be one of the
  following values: Single Step, Multi Step"`). This applies uniformly to
  every field on the test/sample/result-parameter/reference-range/value
  DTOs — a string typed into a numeric column, an invalid enum label, a
  too-short Test Name, etc. all read in plain Excel terms.

  Round-trip an unmodified export through this endpoint to confirm
  `{ created: 0, updated: <all active tests>, skipped: [] }` — a good smoke
  test before trusting an edited file.
}
